---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageHeading from "../components/PageHeading.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About the observatory"
  description="How the lunar phase, lunisolar calendar, and artistic ASCII ocean are calculated."
  ><div class="cream">
    <PageHeading
      eyebrow="Behind the scenes"
      title="A small observatory"
      description="A note on the real calculations, imagined currents, and quiet choices that make this website."
    /><div class="prose page-section">
      <section id="lunar-scene">
        <h2>The moon is real. The sea is imagined.</h2><p>
          Astronomy Engine calculates the current Sun–Moon elongation,
          illuminated fraction, and topocentric moon coordinates. Waxing and
          waning are derived from the full 0–360° phase angle. The reference
          location is Sendai, Japan: 38.2682° N, 140.8694° E, elevation 0 m.
        </p><p>
          The ASCII disc is a procedural sphere with illustrative crater
          texture. It represents the real lunar phase, not a photographic lunar
          map. Its placement is composed for the page, rather than projected
          from the reference sky. Its phase orientation is fixed north-up; local
          sky rotation and libration are not rendered. The reference altitude
          and azimuth are available in the homepage observation notes.
        </p><h3>Two clocks, clearly separated</h3><p>
          Lunar phase follows real-world time and is recalculated every minute
          while the page is visible. The sea’s conceptual lunar and solar
          constituents use periods of 12.4206 and 12 solar hours, accelerated
          2,400 times so their movement can be seen. Three traveling waves add
          swell, cross-wave interference, and ripples. A reflection field
          follows the artistic moon placement and illumination.
        </p><p>
          This is an artistic visualization. It is not calibrated to a
          coastline, has no physical water-level units, and must not be used for
          navigation or tide predictions. Real tides require local bathymetry,
          coastline geometry, and measured harmonic constituents.
        </p><h3>The calendar</h3><p>
          Calendar dates use China / Malaysia time (UTC+8), independent of your
          device’s timezone. The open-source lunar-javascript library converts
          those civil dates to the Chinese lunisolar calendar. A small festival
          registry recognizes Lunar New Year and Mid-Autumn Festival, excluding
          leap months.
        </p><h3>A quieter mode</h3><p>
          The renderer targets 30 frames per second, caps pixel density, lowers
          the character count on small screens, and adapts if drawing is too
          costly. It pauses in hidden tabs and when the scene leaves the
          viewport. Reduced-motion preferences produce a still scene. Without
          JavaScript or Canvas, a pre-rendered ASCII snapshot remains visible;
          its date reflects the last site build.
        </p>
      </section><h2>Made to be kept</h2><p>
        This is a static Astro website hosted on GitHub Pages. Fonts are bundled
        locally: Cormorant Garamond and DM Sans (SIL Open Font License). There
        are no required paid APIs, cookies, analytics, or tracking scripts.
        Public GitHub metadata, when configured, is fetched only during builds
        and can fail without breaking the site.
      </p><p>
        Placeholder entries are labeled and are excluded from the generated
        academic CV. The PDF uses PDFKit, an open-source generator, and shares
        its academic data with the website.
      </p><p>
        <a href="https://github.com/simjiahui/simjiahui.github.io"
          >Explore the source <span class="arrow-icon" aria-hidden="true"
          ></span></a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </div></Layout
>
